**Explanation**: The method iterates through the array, comparing each element to the current maximum and updating it accordingly.

### Exercise 2: Linked List Reversal

**Task**: Implement a method to reverse a linked list.

**Solution**:
```java
public static Node reverseLinkedList(Node head) {
    Node previous = null;
    Node current = head;
    Node next;

    while (current != null) {
        next = current.next; // Store next node
        current.next = previous; // Reverse the link
        previous = current; // Move previous forward
        current = next; // Move current forward
    }
    return previous; // New head of reversed list
}
```
**Explanation**: This method uses three pointers to reverse the links in the linked list.

### Exercise 3: Stack Implementation

**Task**: Implement a basic stack using an array.

**Solution**:
```java
class ArrayStack {
    private int[] stack;
    private int top;
    private int capacity;

    ArrayStack(int size) {
        stack = new int[size];
        capacity = size;
        top = -1;
    }

    public void push(int item) {
        if (top == capacity - 1) {
            throw new StackOverflowError("Stack is full");
        }
        stack[++top] = item;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new EmptyStackException();
        }
        return stack[top--];
    }
}
```
**Explanation**: This class represents a stack using an array while managing the top index and ensuring that it does not exceed the capacity.

